  "worked_solution": "Let $x,y,z$ be the dimensions of the rectangular prism, let $S$ be its surface area, and let $V$ be its volume. The variables $x,S$ and $V$ change with time. In particular, we are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =20\\text{\u00a0in}, \\\\ z & =30\\text{\u00a0in}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =10\\text{\u00a0in}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nWe want to find the rate of change of $S$ knowing the rate of change of $x.$ So, we start with the formula for the surface area of the rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nS=2xz+2xy+2yz\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known values, we get\n\n$$\nS=2(30)(x)+2x(20)+2(30)(20)=100x+1\\,200.\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =100(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the above expression using $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=10,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =100(10)=1\\,000{\\text{\u00a0in}}^{2}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rectangular prism's surface area is increasing at a rate of $1\\,000\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}/\\text{min}.$",
